# Settling a comparison's verdict by what the bytes actually say.
#
# The case this exists for is a file whose timestamp moved without its contents changing — which is
# most of a backup drive after a restore, a sync tool, or an unzip. The comparison can only see that
# one side is newer, and copying it back over an identical file is work for nothing.
#
# Note which verdicts are even settleable. A leaf "Differs" means equal timestamps and *unequal
# sizes*, so its bytes cannot be identical and reading them can never change it. Newer, older and
# unknown are the ones that reading answers.

sandbox
mkdir left
mkdir right

# Byte for byte the same, written far enough apart that the comparison calls one of them newer.
# Sandbox.Write fills a file with its own name repeated, so both sides hold identical bytes.
write left/moved-timestamp.txt 400
sleep 2100
write right/moved-timestamp.txt 400

# Genuinely different, and must stay that way — the settlement is not a rubber stamp.
write-text left/really-differs.txt alpha|beta
write-text right/really-differs.txt alpha|gamma

go left
split right right
pane 2
go right
pane 1

compare
shot 01-compared

# Before anything is read: one side is newer, and the other pair genuinely differs.
assert-compare-row moved-timestamp.txt Older
assert-compare-row really-differs.txt Differs

select moved-timestamp.txt, really-differs.txt
settle-content
shot 02-settled

# Identical bytes settle to Same. Different bytes leave the verdict exactly where it was — one
# direction only, and only on proof.
assert-compare-row moved-timestamp.txt Same
assert-compare-row really-differs.txt Differs

echo settled what the bytes agreed on, and nothing else
